WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.006 --> 00:00:04.280
Good enough is great.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:09.961
Perhaps not a concept you'd expect to
hear at the business school, yet this

00:00:09.961 --> 00:00:15.923
notion of good enough is great underlies
a lot of our entrepreneurial efforts.

00:00:15.923 --> 00:00:20.796
The concept of minimally
viable product design

00:00:20.796 --> 00:00:25.560
is critical for
bringing products to market.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:32.958
In this approach, you strive to get your
product or service out there quickly so

00:00:32.958 --> 00:00:38.150
that you can test and get feedback
to see where the true value lies.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.360
Let me give you an example.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.548
Imagine you're a baker, and

00:00:43.548 --> 00:00:48.050
you want to bring your baked
goods to the broad market.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:53.770
You have visions of crafting wonderful
cakes and pies to delight your customers.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:59.634
Minimally viable product design,
MVP would suggest that rather than

00:00:59.634 --> 00:01:03.740
starting with elaborate cakes,
you start by making cupcakes.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:09.690
Cupcakes provide good value
to your potential clients.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.810
They're relatively easy to make.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:17.383
And they allow you to solicit feedback to
better understand how you can hone and

00:01:17.383 --> 00:01:21.190
develop your products and
services for the future.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:26.202
This notion of minimally
viable product design is

00:01:26.202 --> 00:01:32.540
critical to your success in
growing your product in services.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.157
It comes down to five key steps,
understanding the user requirements,

00:01:39.157 --> 00:01:43.915
the problem space or
market that you're trying to deliver in,

00:01:43.915 --> 00:01:48.763
the metrics you'll use to assess
the success of your offering,

00:01:48.763 --> 00:01:54.509
you create designs, really quick product
versions to bring to the market,

00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:57.160
and then you test and you iterate.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:03.662
Fundamentally, the concepts involved
in MVP are things like prototyping,

00:02:03.662 --> 00:02:08.260
understanding the needs and
iteration all to drive you towards value.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.846
The key idea here in our talk
today is this fundamental belief

00:02:15.846 --> 00:02:24.420
that the ideas in minimally viable product
design translate into our communication.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:29.287
So I'd like to welcome you to a talk
on minimally viable communication

00:02:29.287 --> 00:02:32.120
when good enough is great.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.360
My name is Matt Abrahams.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.225
I've been here at the Stanford
Graduate School of Business for

00:02:36.225 --> 00:02:39.370
over a decade,
teaching strategic communication skills.

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:44.193
And I am thrilled to share with you
ways that we can use principles from

00:02:44.193 --> 00:02:48.780
minimally viable product design
to apply to our communication.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:54.164
Much like there were five
steps in the MVP process,

00:02:54.164 --> 00:02:57.850
we have five steps in the MVC process.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:01.509
It starts by looking at your audience,
understanding who they are and

00:03:01.509 --> 00:03:04.390
what they need from your messaging.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:09.056
You have to appreciate the context
in which you're communicating,

00:03:09.056 --> 00:03:14.447
create a goal that is very specific for
that audience in context, structure and

00:03:14.447 --> 00:03:19.370
design out your communication, and
then solicit feedback in iterate.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:25.340
So let's get started and
understand each of these key components.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:29.120
It all begins with your audience.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.700
Who are you speaking to?

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.190
And more importantly,
what are their needs?

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:40.970
Many of us when we communicate,
make a fundamental mistake.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:45.434
And we start from a position
of here's what I want to say,

00:03:45.434 --> 00:03:49.160
rather than what does my
audience need to hear.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:54.186
Now this sounds like verbal jujitsu
where I'm just moving words around, but

00:03:54.186 --> 00:03:57.430
it is a fundamental
difference in our approach.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:03.040
We need to understand our audience and
what they need to hear from us.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.791
So there's several
questions I would like for

00:04:05.791 --> 00:04:09.820
you to think about when you have
important communication to craft.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:15.950
First and foremost,
what does my audience know about my topic?

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:17.330
Do they know a lot?

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:19.030
Or do they know a little?

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:23.696
We have all been victimized by
communicators who start too high level or

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:25.107
too low level for us.

00:04:25.107 --> 00:04:28.693
Second, you need to think
about their attitudes relative

00:04:28.693 --> 00:04:31.700
to the topic that you're discussing.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.550
Are they in favor?

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:35.840
Perhaps they're not in favor
of what you're saying.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:37.030
Or maybe they're agnostic.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:38.690
They just don't know enough.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.130
You also have to think
about resistance points.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:47.600
What are the things that might
cause hesitation in your audience?

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.058
Is it what you're proposing costs
too much money, takes too much time,

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:53.749
requires too much effort?

00:04:53.749 --> 00:04:57.555
We have to think about knowledge,
attitudes and resistance.

00:04:57.555 --> 00:05:00.744
And from my colleagues here
at the business school,

00:05:00.744 --> 00:05:03.500
we add psychological distance.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:06.080
How far are you away from your audience?

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.348
Not physical distance, but
perhaps in an organization,

00:05:09.348 --> 00:05:13.292
are you much more senior or
perhaps more junior to the person talking?

00:05:13.292 --> 00:05:16.820
That matters in terms of what you say and
how you say it.

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.183
According to research from Near Halevy,

00:05:19.183 --> 00:05:23.435
research from Zack Tortola tells us we
have to understand the relevance and

00:05:23.435 --> 00:05:29.130
importance of what we're saying so that we
can motivate and influence our audience.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:34.048
So thinking through our audience is
the first step in the minimally viable

00:05:34.048 --> 00:05:35.802
communication process.

00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:39.860
And this can go badly, in fact,
let me give you an example.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:46.299
There is a durable goods company in
Northern Europe called Electrolux.

00:05:46.299 --> 00:05:50.362
And many years ago,
they ran this ad campaign,

00:05:50.362 --> 00:05:53.421
nothing sucks like an Electrolux.

00:05:53.421 --> 00:05:58.070
This ad was an abysmal failure, why?

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:00.540
We don't like to buy things that suck.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:07.220
You can imagine some advertising person
in their office thinking, I know.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:11.402
We're going to suggest that our
vacuum cleaner suction is so

00:06:11.402 --> 00:06:14.850
powerful that it caused
the Tower of Pisa to leak.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:19.752
But they didn't understand their
audience down to the linguistic level,

00:06:19.752 --> 00:06:22.099
nothing sucks like an Electrolux.

00:06:22.099 --> 00:06:26.996
So we have to take the time as part of
our design process to think through

00:06:26.996 --> 00:06:30.660
our audience at many levels
including linguistic.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:36.123
Now let me share an example of where
understanding your audience actually was

00:06:36.123 --> 00:06:37.410
very helpful.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:42.255
And I borrowed this story from where I
first read it in the book Made to Stick by

00:06:42.255 --> 00:06:46.891
Dan and Chip Heath, and Chip Heath
teaches here at the business school.

00:06:46.891 --> 00:06:51.850
And in this inmate distinct,
they describe the story of Texas.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:57.399
In Texas in the mid 1980s,
there was a huge littering problem,

00:06:57.399 --> 00:07:00.950
trash and rubbish all over the place.

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:03.360
It didn't look good,
and it wasn't healthy.

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:10.480
So the government invested in a public
service campaign, featuring this guy.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.864
And I know many of you listening
in today are of my vintage and

00:07:13.864 --> 00:07:18.430
you might remember Woodsy the Owl,
give a hoot, don't pollute.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:21.540
And Woodsy was placed on billboards,
television, and radio.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:24.531
Remember this was the 80s,
there was no internet.

00:07:24.531 --> 00:07:27.029
When I share this story
with my MBA students today,

00:07:27.029 --> 00:07:30.060
they physically get disturbed
at the thought of no internet.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:36.003
Something interesting happened though,
after two years of Woodsy

00:07:36.003 --> 00:07:40.940
being on television, radio, and
billboards, littering got worse.

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:44.998
It was as if Texans were saying,
we don't like you Woody, and

00:07:44.998 --> 00:07:47.530
they were throwing more
garbage all over the place.

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.314
This caused great concern.

00:07:50.314 --> 00:07:54.404
So the government invested in
an effort to bring academics in to

00:07:54.404 --> 00:07:56.655
help with the littering problem.

00:07:56.655 --> 00:07:59.870
And the very first thing
the academics did,

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:02.640
Was try to figure out who
is doing the littering.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:06.660
Now, for some of you,
this might not be surprising.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:10.860
It turns out that most of the littering
was being perpetrated by men.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:15.597
Not just any men but
men between the ages of 18 and 34.

00:08:15.597 --> 00:08:18.751
And they went further beyond learning
who was doing the littering,

00:08:18.751 --> 00:08:22.249
they looked into some of the attitudes and
beliefs that these people held.

00:08:22.249 --> 00:08:25.810
And for any of you who have been
to Texas or grew up in Texas,

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:30.800
you know one thing is true about
Texas males in that age demographic.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:33.901
They are very proud of being from Texas.

00:08:33.901 --> 00:08:36.430
If they could,
they would make Texas its own country.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:40.428
Based on knowing who was
doing the littering and

00:08:40.428 --> 00:08:44.410
some of the attitudes,
they created this ad campaign.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:46.810
If you've been to Texas, you've seen it.

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:49.970
It's still around today, 30 years later.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.649
Don't Mess With Texas.

00:08:52.649 --> 00:08:57.001
It's a play on words, don't mess,
meaning literally don't make a mess,

00:08:57.001 --> 00:08:59.450
but also don't mess, I take great pride.

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:04.234
Within two years of this
ad campaign coming out,

00:09:04.234 --> 00:09:08.541
they were able to reduce littering
in the state to up to 80%.

00:09:08.541 --> 00:09:13.371
If you know anything about
social science research,

00:09:13.371 --> 00:09:17.028
an effect size that large is unheard of.

00:09:17.028 --> 00:09:21.929
By getting the right message to
the right people in the right way,

00:09:21.929 --> 00:09:23.988
you can have a huge effect.

00:09:23.988 --> 00:09:28.446
The take home message here is
take time to know your audience,

00:09:28.446 --> 00:09:33.433
it is the first step in the minimally
viable communication process.

00:09:33.433 --> 00:09:36.115
But it's not enough just to
know about your audience,

00:09:36.115 --> 00:09:39.960
you also have to think about the context
in which you are communicating.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:43.870
Whenever you communicate, there's
a context which includes many things.

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:48.300
Cultural elements, channel elements
like the technology you're using.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.781
But I want to highlight
two that are critical for

00:09:50.781 --> 00:09:53.960
bringing communication to your audience.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:56.980
The first has to do with the time
of day you are communicating.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.505
Right now it's my morning.

00:09:59.505 --> 00:10:03.672
For some of you, it's your early afternoon
and for others, it's your evening.

00:10:03.672 --> 00:10:07.224
That impacts what you bring to
our communication, your focus,

00:10:07.224 --> 00:10:10.270
your willingness to collaborate,
your engagement.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:15.287
Research by GSB Professor Baba Shiv
looks at our

00:10:15.287 --> 00:10:20.109
neuro-chemical profile,
depending on the time of day.

00:10:20.109 --> 00:10:23.637
In fact his research suggests,
if you're looking for

00:10:23.637 --> 00:10:28.318
collaborative work, it's best to do
it early in the morning when people

00:10:28.318 --> 00:10:33.020
are more open to collaboration based
on dopamine and serotonin levels.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:37.640
Now that doesn't mean the only time
to collaborate is in the morning.

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.745
What it means though is if you're
doing it later in the day,

00:10:40.745 --> 00:10:42.917
you might have to do
some team building and

00:10:42.917 --> 00:10:47.940
trust building activities first to get
people to that place of collaboration.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:51.510
Time of day matters when it
comes to your communication.

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:57.250
Another timing variable involved
in context is sequence.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:02.530
We often prepare our communication as
if it will be received in isolation.

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:06.670
Yet we all live lives where we realize
we are constantly bombarded with

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:08.927
different communication messages.

00:11:08.927 --> 00:11:12.009
Think about the sequence
of your messaging,

00:11:12.009 --> 00:11:16.560
where are you in the interaction
that your audience experiences?

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:23.127
Think of it this way, if you're proposing
a new plan that involves investment

00:11:23.127 --> 00:11:27.862
from your organization and
you're going into the executive committee,

00:11:27.862 --> 00:11:30.764
following somebody who delivered bad news,

00:11:30.764 --> 00:11:34.150
might that influence
the decision making process?

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:37.300
It could,
you need to think about that sequencing?

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:41.354
Similarly, what could you do to set
up others who come behind you and

00:11:41.354 --> 00:11:44.390
follow you in your communication?

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:47.420
So context is equally
critical to think about.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:53.602
I recently heard this quote that I love
that really reinforces the value of

00:11:53.602 --> 00:11:59.020
thinking about your audience and
your context, it goes something like this.

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:04.123
Writing a high stakes communication
without thinking about your audience

00:12:04.123 --> 00:12:09.770
is like writing a love letter and
addressing it to whom it may concern.

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:11.570
You would never do that.

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:14.886
Nor should you draft high stakes
communication without thinking about your

00:12:14.886 --> 00:12:15.410
audience.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:21.966
Into that idea I add, without thinking
about the context it's like taking

00:12:21.966 --> 00:12:27.050
that love letter, putting it in a bottle,
throwing it in the ocean hoping

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:31.816
it lands in the right place,
you must equally focus on your context.

00:12:31.816 --> 00:12:33.890
So the question becomes why, and

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:37.826
what's next in the minimally
viable communication process?

00:12:37.826 --> 00:12:42.756
Once we think about audience and context,
we have to think about our goal.

00:12:42.756 --> 00:12:46.774
All high stakes communication
must be goal driven, and

00:12:46.774 --> 00:12:50.430
to my mind a goal has
three major components.

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:54.446
It's about information,
emotion, and action.

00:12:54.446 --> 00:12:57.973
In other words,
what do you want your audience to know?

00:12:57.973 --> 00:13:02.263
How do you want them to feel,
and what do you want them to do?

00:13:02.263 --> 00:13:05.340
Know, feel, do.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:09.380
Your goal is predicated on what you know
about your audience and your context.

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:11.477
So if the audience changes or
the context changes,

00:13:11.477 --> 00:13:12.950
you might need to change your goal.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:18.990
For this presentation I'm giving
you today, my goal is quite simple.

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:22.788
I'd like for
you to leave here knowing how minimally

00:13:22.788 --> 00:13:27.460
viable product design processes
apply to your communication.

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:31.340
Further, I'd like you to
know the specific steps and

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:35.530
best practices in the minimally
viable communication process.

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:39.639
I want you to feel confident in your
ability to apply these processes and

00:13:39.639 --> 00:13:44.366
ultimately to put them into practice
whenever you're bringing messaging to your

00:13:44.366 --> 00:13:45.348
audiences.

00:13:45.348 --> 00:13:48.970
Know, feel, do.

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:52.353
Take the time to figure out
your communication goal,

00:13:52.353 --> 00:13:55.570
because this goal will come
back to help us later.

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:59.866
Not only does it direct what
we want to say going forward,

00:13:59.866 --> 00:14:02.650
it also helps us assess our success.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:08.576
It is critically important that
you take time to understand what

00:14:08.576 --> 00:14:13.180
the success of your
communication would look like.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:17.958
When I travel all over the world teaching
communication skills, I'll often ask,

00:14:17.958 --> 00:14:21.680
how do you know if your
communication was successful?

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:25.816
And the number one answer I get is,
I got through it,

00:14:25.816 --> 00:14:30.200
as if survival is the key
metric of success.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:31.930
Of course, I think that's ridiculous.

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:34.844
You should be able to measure
the success of your messaging,

00:14:34.844 --> 00:14:38.230
your communication,
based on the goal you set out with.

00:14:38.230 --> 00:14:42.912
So a goal not only helps you figure out
what direction to take your messaging,

00:14:42.912 --> 00:14:45.300
it also helps you assess your success.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:51.600
Now once you have a goal in mind,
we then have to spend time ideating

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:55.680
before we can create our structure,
we have to be inspired to

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:01.080
think about what are the key themes and
ideas that I want to get across.

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:03.653
And this is where lessons
from Stanford's D school,

00:15:03.653 --> 00:15:06.340
design school, can really come in handy.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:10.750
There are myriad ways to get
creative in coming up with your

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:13.904
ideas that will feed into your messages.

00:15:13.904 --> 00:15:18.464
Let me share a few that I like to use
as I'm creating my minimally viable

00:15:18.464 --> 00:15:19.636
communication.

00:15:19.636 --> 00:15:24.263
First and foremost there's old fashioned
brainstorming, most of us have done that.

00:15:24.263 --> 00:15:28.524
When you just generate ideas without
bringing judgment to them at first,

00:15:28.524 --> 00:15:31.890
it's a good idea to give
you some time constraints.

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:34.740
Research suggests time constraints
help with brainstorming.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:38.910
But other techniques work well too.

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:43.080
One I'll often employ is to put
myself in a different perspective.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:49.767
I'll ask myself how would a seven
year old Matt address this issue?

00:15:49.767 --> 00:15:53.825
Kids are wonderfully creative, put
yourself in that creative playful mindset

00:15:53.825 --> 00:15:57.458
and ask yourself how you would
approach this particular communication

00:15:57.458 --> 00:16:00.500
challenge with the goal you have and
where would that lead?

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:06.530
Another way I like to brainstorm or
come up with ideas is to do a mind map.

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:11.616
So rather than listing things out which
can lead to chronological bullet point,

00:16:11.616 --> 00:16:12.900
ideas and results.

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:16.043
Rather get graphic,
draw circles and arrows and

00:16:16.043 --> 00:16:19.270
see if that can help you
come up with unique ideas.

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:25.131
And perhaps my most favorite way of
generating ideas is one I recently learned

00:16:25.131 --> 00:16:27.092
from a colleague at the D school.

00:16:27.092 --> 00:16:29.230
And that is using analogies,

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:35.055
think about situations that are similar
to what you're trying to communicate.

00:16:35.055 --> 00:16:39.050
And what could you learn by exploring
those different situations.

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:42.374
Let me give you two examples of
what I mean by this, thinking and

00:16:42.374 --> 00:16:44.020
creativity through analogy.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.223
There was a hospital chain in
the Midwest that was interested in

00:16:50.223 --> 00:16:54.270
improving their emergency room results.

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:56.798
And rather than going around
to other hospitals and

00:16:56.798 --> 00:17:00.109
checking their best practices,
which I'm sure they likely did.

00:17:00.109 --> 00:17:05.289
They actually went to a Formula One
pit crew and they observed what

00:17:05.289 --> 00:17:10.101
a pit crew did to quickly get
cars back out on the race course.

00:17:10.101 --> 00:17:13.299
And if you think about it,
a racing pit crew and

00:17:13.299 --> 00:17:16.715
what happens in the Is really similar.

00:17:16.715 --> 00:17:19.375
You've got time critical, sensitive,

00:17:19.375 --> 00:17:23.925
urgent issues that have to be fixed and
resolved quickly.

00:17:23.925 --> 00:17:27.036
And they learn many things by looking
at how the pit crew function.

00:17:27.036 --> 00:17:30.486
For example,
most pit crews have kits set up for

00:17:30.486 --> 00:17:34.505
different situations they
might find themselves in.

00:17:34.505 --> 00:17:39.223
So let's say a tire blows out, they have
a whole set of materials set aside for

00:17:39.223 --> 00:17:41.050
dealing with that one issue.

00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:45.497
The hospitals in their ER's took
that idea and developed kits for

00:17:45.497 --> 00:17:48.110
the different types of
emergencies they might see.

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:53.975
Another example is Apple,
when Apple opened up its retail stores,

00:17:53.975 --> 00:17:59.149
they created the genius bars essentially
the tech support in the stores.

00:17:59.149 --> 00:18:04.905
Rather than or in addition to looking
at top notch service organizations.

00:18:04.905 --> 00:18:11.040
They actually went and explored how high
end hotel concierge services worked.

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:17.568
By using analogies, new ideas and
approaches can come to mind.

00:18:17.568 --> 00:18:24.410
And this ideation can then help you
begin to create your communication.

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:27.932
So once you have your goal, which is
based on your audience and context,

00:18:27.932 --> 00:18:30.082
you have to spend some time brainstorming.

00:18:30.082 --> 00:18:34.088
And coming up with the information that
you want to communicate that will help you

00:18:34.088 --> 00:18:35.120
achieve your goal.

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:40.646
Once you have those ideas in mind,
you have to crystallize them and

00:18:40.646 --> 00:18:43.170
bring them into clear focus.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:50.530
And I love the tool of 6 word stories
to help bring focus to your ideas.

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:56.397
Take all of the themes and
the creativity that you spent in

00:18:56.397 --> 00:19:01.295
the ideation phase and
try to distil them down in 6 words.

00:19:01.295 --> 00:19:05.398
And that 6 word narrative
becomes the driver for

00:19:05.398 --> 00:19:11.416
the message design that you will create,
this is a focusing activity.

00:19:11.416 --> 00:19:17.233
For this talk today,
my 6 word story is craft messages for

00:19:17.233 --> 00:19:20.090
quick iterative feedback.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:26.510
That was the narrative I used to help
me structure this entire presentation.

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:33.214
What's your 6 word story for
the messages you need to create?

00:19:33.214 --> 00:19:38.265
The next step in the minimally
viable communication process,

00:19:38.265 --> 00:19:44.090
once we've ideated and we've focused,
is to structure our messages.

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:50.596
Structure is absolutely critical for
your communication,

00:19:50.596 --> 00:19:55.900
we have all been victimized by
people who ramble on and on.

00:19:55.900 --> 00:20:00.657
I hope that's not happening right now,
when somebody is rambling,

00:20:00.657 --> 00:20:03.947
it makes it really challenging for
us to focus.

00:20:03.947 --> 00:20:08.350
We have to bring a lot of energy to focus
and what most of us do is we check out.

00:20:08.350 --> 00:20:14.583
So what is it that we can do to
make it easier for our audience?

00:20:14.583 --> 00:20:19.927
And that thing is to make sure
that you provide structure,

00:20:19.927 --> 00:20:22.664
I learnt this a long time ago.

00:20:22.664 --> 00:20:26.723
As an undergrad at Stanford,
I was a tour guide, and

00:20:26.723 --> 00:20:31.060
one of the number one things
they taught us as a tour guide

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:35.690
on the Stanford campus is
never lose your audience.

00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:38.950
You are a bad tour guide
if you get people lost,

00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:42.930
how do good tour guides
keep people together?

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:47.026
They set their expectations and
that's one of the powers of structure,

00:20:47.026 --> 00:20:49.800
structure sets your expectation.

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:52.714
Would any of you go on a tour with me
if I showed up and said, Hi, I'm Matt,

00:20:52.714 --> 00:20:54.080
I'm your tour guide, let's go?

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:58.336
Some of you would because you're
adventurous but many of you would be like,

00:20:58.336 --> 00:20:59.540
heck no, where are we going?

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:00.370
Why are we going there?

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:02.340
Do I have the right shoes on?

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:06.826
So by setting your expectations up front,
by structuring your tour,

00:21:06.826 --> 00:21:08.430
you can relax and enjoy.

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:15.669
The other value that structure brings,
is structure helps us

00:21:15.669 --> 00:21:20.720
to remember the information
that's being communicated.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:25.012
Neuroscientists tell us that structured
information is actually processed more

00:21:25.012 --> 00:21:26.510
fluently and efficiently.

00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:28.899
Think about this,

00:21:28.899 --> 00:21:32.680
how many of you can remember phone
numbers from when you were a kid?

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:37.490
Most of us can, now my kids today,
they don't have to remember phone numbers.

00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:40.221
They just look at a picture and
push a button on their phone and

00:21:40.221 --> 00:21:41.677
somebody else starts talking.

00:21:41.677 --> 00:21:44.972
But in our day, we had to remember phone
numbers, here in the United States,

00:21:44.972 --> 00:21:47.450
our phone numbers are 10 digits long.

00:21:47.450 --> 00:21:51.718
It's hard to remember 10 digits,
if I gave you 10 numbers right now and

00:21:51.718 --> 00:21:53.977
asked you to remember them for an hour.

00:21:53.977 --> 00:21:58.094
Or a day, or a year or a decade or
in my case many decades,

00:21:58.094 --> 00:22:01.770
that would be hard, so how do we do it?

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:05.631
We leverage structure,
we don't remember 10 digits, rather,

00:22:05.631 --> 00:22:09.437
here in the United States,
we remember the three digit area code.

00:22:09.437 --> 00:22:15.002
The three digit prefix and the four digit
number, that structure helps us remember.

00:22:15.002 --> 00:22:21.304
So structure is something that can be
very useful to you as you communicate.

00:22:21.304 --> 00:22:24.549
So, allow me to introduce you
to one of many structures and

00:22:24.549 --> 00:22:27.408
when it comes to minimally
viable communication.

00:22:27.408 --> 00:22:32.959
I believe this structure is perhaps
the most effective one you can use and

00:22:32.959 --> 00:22:37.242
this structure is, the what?,
so what?, now what?

00:22:37.242 --> 00:22:41.674
Structure, let me explain it and then let
me share with you how you can use it.

00:22:41.674 --> 00:22:45.871
In this structure you start by talking
about what it is you are crafting ,it's

00:22:45.871 --> 00:22:46.697
your message,

00:22:46.697 --> 00:22:51.100
your idea ,your product ,your service,
whatever it is you are communicating.

00:22:52.310 --> 00:22:54.720
The so what is why is it important or

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:58.308
relevant to the audience
that you are speaking to?

00:22:58.308 --> 00:23:02.048
It could be to the audience directly,
it could be to the company,

00:23:02.048 --> 00:23:05.040
it could be to society,
but it involves relevance.

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:07.798
And then finally, now what?

00:23:07.798 --> 00:23:09.500
What comes next?

00:23:09.500 --> 00:23:14.196
It has momentum built in, maybe the next
step is to watch a demonstration.

00:23:14.196 --> 00:23:21.350
To take a taste of something, to set up
a next meeting, what?, so what?, now what?

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:24.710
Is a powerful way of
structuring your messages, why?

00:23:24.710 --> 00:23:27.610
It's easier to remember,
you simply answer the three questions and

00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:29.240
you get a structure.

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:31.513
Second, it has that relevance built in and

00:23:31.513 --> 00:23:34.870
if something is relevant people
will pay more attention to it.

00:23:34.870 --> 00:23:37.689
And they're more likely to remember and
act on it, and

00:23:37.689 --> 00:23:39.910
it has that momentum, that now what step.

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:44.150
Helping you understand what
to do after the message.

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:49.673
So, as you are quickly crafting your
content to bring to your audience to

00:23:49.673 --> 00:23:53.250
iterate, what?, so what?,
now what?, is your friend.

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:58.720
I'd like to take a meta moment here,
a timeout.

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:03.560
In my MBA classes, we do a lot
of this meta moment stuff, why?

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:07.020
It allows us to better
understand our communication and

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:12.170
I just want to point out that I just
explained what, so what, now what?

00:24:12.170 --> 00:24:18.760
Using what, so what, now what, it's a
powerful concept for teaching information.

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:22.080
In fact, everything I've shared today
has been packaged in this structure.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:25.484
I tell you what it is,
why it's important and

00:24:25.484 --> 00:24:28.360
share with you how you can best use it.

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:32.910
So I encourage you to, once you've
spent some time getting creative idea,

00:24:32.910 --> 00:24:37.319
in focusing your message to think about
how can I structure that message to

00:24:37.319 --> 00:24:41.110
achieve that six word story over
arching approach that I have.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:47.670
The final phase in our
minimally viable communication

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:51.480
process is to search for feedback.

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:56.099
The whole goal of minimally viable
communication just like minimally

00:24:56.099 --> 00:25:00.873
viable product design is to iterate,
you need to test and get feedback so

00:25:00.873 --> 00:25:03.080
that you can hone your messages.

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:06.149
Here you see on the screen,

00:25:06.149 --> 00:25:11.200
something I remember from a kid as part
of a science lab in elementary school.

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:16.112
We had to build contraptions that would
allow an egg to fall from a rooftop

00:25:16.112 --> 00:25:18.379
to the ground without cracking.

00:25:18.379 --> 00:25:20.988
And we tested a lot of different ideas.

00:25:20.988 --> 00:25:26.508
The winning idea was a parachute strapped
to the egg and that's what you see here,

00:25:26.508 --> 00:25:31.548
we have to test, we have to iterate,
we have to ask for feedback and based on

00:25:31.548 --> 00:25:37.148
that feedback, go through this process
again, and again until we have the message

00:25:37.148 --> 00:25:42.130
honed in such a way that it can provide
the optimal value for our audience.

00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:48.969
The minimally viable communication
design process can help you in many,

00:25:48.969 --> 00:25:51.874
if not most, communication situations.

00:25:51.874 --> 00:25:57.917
It starts by understanding your audience,
their knowledge level, their attitudes,

00:25:57.917 --> 00:26:02.819
their resistance points,
the psychological distance between you and

00:26:02.819 --> 00:26:08.750
them, and the relevance and
importance of what you're saying to them.

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:12.805
We have to think about the context, which
includes many facets, including timing,

00:26:12.805 --> 00:26:14.000
time of day in sequence.

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:19.921
You have to spend time understanding
your goal, know, feel, do.

00:26:19.921 --> 00:26:24.155
And that goal allows you not only to
focus your messaging and efforts, but

00:26:24.155 --> 00:26:29.650
it also allows you to assess your success
in the final phase, that feedback phase.

00:26:29.650 --> 00:26:32.320
And then we have to take
the time to structure, but

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:35.830
before we can structure we
have to ideate and focus.

00:26:35.830 --> 00:26:39.939
Taken together,
this process of minimally viable

00:26:39.939 --> 00:26:44.907
communication design can help
you get meaningful messages to

00:26:44.907 --> 00:26:49.890
your audiences very quickly to
add value for you and for them.

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:55.471
If you'd like to learn more about
this type of communication, or

00:26:55.471 --> 00:26:59.910
other aspects of communication,
I'd like to point you to a few resources.

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:04.472
I've written a book called speaking up
without freaking out that can help you

00:27:04.472 --> 00:27:07.810
feel more comfortable and
confident in your communication.

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:10.892
I curate a website called
NoFreakingSpeaking.com

00:27:10.892 --> 00:27:15.390
where my research and that of others
is there for you to check out.

00:27:15.390 --> 00:27:20.176
And one of the greatest pleasures of
my life is I get to host a podcast for

00:27:20.176 --> 00:27:25.775
the business school called Think Fast,
Talk Smart, where if we do our job right,

00:27:25.775 --> 00:27:31.540
we use minimally viable communication to
bring relevant specific communication,

00:27:31.540 --> 00:27:38.160
tools, and ideas to you from Stanford
faculty, mainly from the business school.

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:43.604
I invite all of you to continue to develop
and hone your communication skills.

00:27:43.604 --> 00:27:48.070
And remember, good enough is great.

00:27:48.070 --> 00:27:53.078
And with that, I'd now like to take some
time to listen to your questions and

00:27:53.078 --> 00:27:57.630
ideas around how to become
a more effective communicator.

00:27:57.630 --> 00:28:00.969
I'll agree on do we have some
questions that have come in.

00:28:00.969 --> 00:28:05.226
&gt;&gt; We do.
So I had a question come in asking.

00:28:05.226 --> 00:28:09.520
When it comes to leadership, a lot of
leadership coaches told us that although

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:14.009
leaders think that they have explained
enough their audience feels that they have

00:28:14.009 --> 00:28:16.590
not received enough communication.

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:21.819
So how is that affected by a leader
trying to be minimally viable?

00:28:21.819 --> 00:28:26.064
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I appreciate the insight
motivating that question which is

00:28:26.064 --> 00:28:30.246
minimally viable doesn't
necessarily mean less information.

00:28:30.246 --> 00:28:35.017
Minimally viable simply means that
you're testing out your messages to

00:28:35.017 --> 00:28:38.157
understand what's needed for
your audience.

00:28:38.157 --> 00:28:43.089
So I actually think minimally viable
communication resolves the problem that

00:28:43.089 --> 00:28:45.400
the questioner is asking.

00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:49.621
Because as a leader, if I have important
information to share, I go out and

00:28:49.621 --> 00:28:54.445
iterate and test my messaging with some of
the people in the audience and understand,

00:28:54.445 --> 00:28:55.587
am I saying enough?

00:28:55.587 --> 00:28:56.940
Am I saying too much?

00:28:56.940 --> 00:28:58.856
Is there more I need to bring?

00:28:58.856 --> 00:29:03.220
And that will help me hone my message for
the broader audience.

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:05.530
In other words, I make those cupcakes and

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:09.130
I bring those cupcakes to
a few people in that audience.

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:11.370
I get their taste test and information and

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:15.690
then I prepare myself to bring the big
sheet cake to everybody in the audience.

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:19.650
So I actually think minimally viable
communication is the solution to

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:22.210
the problem that
the questioner was asking.

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:25.250
&gt;&gt; Our next question is,

00:29:25.250 --> 00:29:29.975
isn't the TED Talk mentality
an enemy of your proposed approach?

00:29:29.975 --> 00:29:32.590
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Polished, endlessly reach,

00:29:32.590 --> 00:29:34.510
rehearsed to the point of perfection.

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:40.500
&gt;&gt; Great, I love the challenge
that this is bringing in.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:42.330
So when it comes to communication,

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:45.470
I separate communication into
two different approaches.

00:29:45.470 --> 00:29:48.480
One is the planned communication.

00:29:48.480 --> 00:29:51.910
And that's this TED Talk-like
approach where you plan,

00:29:51.910 --> 00:29:56.060
you practice, you really try
to dial in the communication.

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:59.456
And then there is the more
spontaneous communication,

00:29:59.456 --> 00:30:04.290
the communication that happens
off the cuff in the moment, etc.

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:09.142
And this approach, this mental approach
minimally viable communication can play

00:30:09.142 --> 00:30:13.643
out in both sections, in both approaches
to your communication, clearly,

00:30:13.643 --> 00:30:18.492
in the plan presenting you're practicing,
you're iterating, you're getting

00:30:18.492 --> 00:30:23.162
feedback and you're leveraging a lot of
the tools that I mentioned in the talk.

00:30:23.162 --> 00:30:27.561
In a spontaneous speaking situation,
again, most of our communication,

00:30:27.561 --> 00:30:31.000
you can still leverage
many of these principles.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:33.260
Immediately think about your audience and
their needs.

00:30:33.260 --> 00:30:35.530
Consider the context you're speaking in.

00:30:35.530 --> 00:30:37.753
What is my goal in this moment?

00:30:37.753 --> 00:30:40.729
And then structure messages to answer it.

00:30:40.729 --> 00:30:44.520
So the same process can
apply in real time.

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:47.610
When the question I just answered came in,
I immediately thought,

00:30:47.610 --> 00:30:48.540
who am I talking to?

00:30:48.540 --> 00:30:51.300
I'm talking to a bunch of alumnus
from the business school.

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:54.090
I thought about this context
of it being virtual, and

00:30:54.090 --> 00:30:58.760
the situation where you're hoping to
learn something of value you can apply.

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:00.180
And I immediately thought of my goal.

00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:03.414
What do I want you to know, feel and
do as a result of my answer,

00:31:03.414 --> 00:31:06.360
to your question and
that fed into the way I answered it.

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:11.626
So, in other words, I applied these
specific, the processes I went through,

00:31:11.626 --> 00:31:16.515
into how I handled the spontaneous
situation of answering the question.

00:31:16.515 --> 00:31:21.060
So the answer boils down to there
are two fundamental different types of

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:23.795
communication, spontaneous and plain.

00:31:24.945 --> 00:31:27.640
And what we talked about applies to both.

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:32.184
Now I heard some value judgment
in that question, which is,

00:31:32.184 --> 00:31:37.670
isn't that TED Talkie overly rehearsed,
scripted approach?

00:31:37.670 --> 00:31:43.050
Something that is not to be desired in
perhaps everyday business communication.

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:45.914
Well, I think at the extremes
that's probably true, but

00:31:45.914 --> 00:31:49.595
the notion of being planned, prepared,
and iterating I think can help for

00:31:49.595 --> 00:31:51.650
both planned and spontaneous speaking.

00:31:54.042 --> 00:31:56.042
Think this might be related
to what you just said and

00:31:56.042 --> 00:31:57.860
you may have already answered it.

00:31:57.860 --> 00:32:03.153
But, how does the ideation,
iteration and practice,

00:32:03.153 --> 00:32:07.450
practice practice, effect authenticity?

00:32:07.450 --> 00:32:10.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so authenticity is critical.

00:32:10.150 --> 00:32:13.800
So when it comes to your presence.

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:17.960
It's really about confidence,
credibility and authenticity.

00:32:18.970 --> 00:32:23.662
And you want to walk that
fine line of being prepared,

00:32:23.662 --> 00:32:26.970
but not being over prepared.

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:30.289
And that's why in the work I do here
at the business school in terms of

00:32:30.289 --> 00:32:33.470
the strategic communication classes,
I teach and co-teach.

00:32:33.470 --> 00:32:36.790
It's all about teaching
fundamental best practices.

00:32:36.790 --> 00:32:42.850
And giving students opportunities to
explore and test those best practices.

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:46.840
So that you can then
internalize what works for you.

00:32:46.840 --> 00:32:50.060
So you can develop your own style.

00:32:50.060 --> 00:32:53.290
I like to use the analogy from sports.

00:32:53.290 --> 00:32:57.151
All of us at some point in our life
learned how to swing some kind of sporting

00:32:57.151 --> 00:32:58.140
implement.

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:00.035
A bat, a club, a racket and

00:33:00.035 --> 00:33:05.491
we learn some foundational principles
that will help us be good at doing that.

00:33:05.491 --> 00:33:09.171
But if I were to look at any of you
swinging one of those implements,

00:33:09.171 --> 00:33:13.708
you wouldn't look slightly different than
the way I do it the way other people do it

00:33:13.708 --> 00:33:17.490
based on your experience,
your expertise, your age or injuries.

00:33:18.800 --> 00:33:23.670
It is finding the foundational
principles and honing them for you.

00:33:23.670 --> 00:33:26.060
That leads to your authenticity.

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:30.415
So these foundational principles that
I covered today audience, context,

00:33:30.415 --> 00:33:33.363
goal, ideation, focus,
structure, feedback,

00:33:33.363 --> 00:33:37.180
those foundational principles
you have to internalize.

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:41.641
Take from different areas and different
experiences hone it for yourself and

00:33:41.641 --> 00:33:45.085
that will allow you to be more
authentic as you deploy them.

00:33:45.085 --> 00:33:49.933
So I certainly don't want everybody
to speak the same way to structure

00:33:49.933 --> 00:33:51.588
messages the same way.

00:33:51.588 --> 00:33:55.640
But I do think there is value by going
through a fundamental process to help you

00:33:55.640 --> 00:33:57.280
come up with those messages.

00:33:57.280 --> 00:34:02.168
I think that only bolsters your
authenticity rather than taking

00:34:02.168 --> 00:34:03.300
away from it.

00:34:03.300 --> 00:34:04.170
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:07.640
We have a question here asking
what happens after MVC?

00:34:07.640 --> 00:34:10.696
How does one then do
the full on communication?

00:34:10.696 --> 00:34:15.117
&gt;&gt; Thank you so this is only
the beginning this is to help you

00:34:15.117 --> 00:34:19.742
figure out what to say you must
then focus on how you say it.

00:34:19.742 --> 00:34:23.145
And you have to focus on the channel
through which you deliver it.

00:34:23.145 --> 00:34:27.020
So we have to think first
I have this message.

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:29.950
What's the best way to get
that message to my audience?

00:34:29.950 --> 00:34:31.880
Is it a multi-channel approach?

00:34:31.880 --> 00:34:35.010
Is it an individual conversation or
dialogue?

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:38.470
So the next step is to think about
how do I deploy that message.

00:34:38.470 --> 00:34:41.540
And again, you can iterate and
tests and figure that out.

00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:46.256
And then you also have to focus on your
nonverbal presence how do you actually

00:34:46.256 --> 00:34:50.614
deliver the message you can have
the best well crafted honed message.

00:34:50.614 --> 00:34:53.518
But if it's delivered poorly that can get
in the way so I'm going to wax poetic just

00:34:53.518 --> 00:34:56.230
for a couple minutes because I have
a lot of thoughts on nonverbal presence.

00:34:56.230 --> 00:35:01.900
First, you have to make sure you
are comfortable and confident.

00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:05.462
And some of the resources I pointed to at
the end of what I said can help you in

00:35:05.462 --> 00:35:06.690
that way.

00:35:06.690 --> 00:35:10.982
There are many tools that you can use to
feel more comfortable and confident in

00:35:10.982 --> 00:35:14.960
your communication and I encourage
you to take the time to work on that.

00:35:14.960 --> 00:35:19.088
In all the strategic communication classes
taught here at the business school,

00:35:19.088 --> 00:35:20.980
we start with confidence building.

00:35:22.130 --> 00:35:25.576
Once you have a foundational layer of
confidence, level of confidence in what

00:35:25.576 --> 00:35:29.190
you're doing, you then have to think
about how you show up to deliver.

00:35:29.190 --> 00:35:33.830
And I'd like to give you three bits of
advice about your nonverbal presence.

00:35:33.830 --> 00:35:36.160
First is what you do with your body.

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:38.360
There are three key
concepts with your body.

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:43.510
You want to be big, you want to be
balanced and you want to be still.

00:35:43.510 --> 00:35:46.774
Nervous people make themselves small,

00:35:46.774 --> 00:35:51.670
they retreat in person I step back,
in a virtual world.

00:35:51.670 --> 00:35:54.900
I sit far away so
you don't see my face as much.

00:35:54.900 --> 00:35:56.750
We want to be big and present.

00:35:56.750 --> 00:36:00.858
A great way to help yourself be big is
simply to take your shoulder blades,

00:36:00.858 --> 00:36:02.249
your scapula, bones and

00:36:02.249 --> 00:36:06.509
your back Pull them down when I pull them
down towards my waist I get broader.

00:36:06.509 --> 00:36:09.015
Now I'm not bringing my elbows back and
puffing out my chest rather I'm

00:36:09.015 --> 00:36:11.799
just pulling those shoulder blades down
that helps me look big and confident.

00:36:11.799 --> 00:36:14.796
It also tightens up
the muscles in my neck so

00:36:14.796 --> 00:36:19.710
my head is straight many of us tilt
our heads and tilting your heads when

00:36:19.710 --> 00:36:25.070
you're in one on one interaction listening
is actually a great thing to do.

00:36:25.070 --> 00:36:28.579
But when you're trying to command
confidence delivering an important

00:36:28.579 --> 00:36:31.454
message, you want your head
to be straight, not tilted.

00:36:31.454 --> 00:36:36.169
And this body posturing will help and
clearly shifting and rocking and swaying

00:36:36.169 --> 00:36:41.250
can be very distracting, so big, balanced
and still in terms of your body posture.

00:36:42.300 --> 00:36:46.838
The second thing to think about
is eye contact in North American

00:36:46.838 --> 00:36:51.633
culture where I am today eye contact
is expected we expect people to

00:36:51.633 --> 00:36:56.274
look us in the eye when I'm in
person I will segment my audience.

00:36:56.274 --> 00:37:00.415
Instead of trying to look at every person
individually, I'll create quadrants and

00:37:00.415 --> 00:37:03.696
I'll look in the back part of the room or
the front part of the room.

00:37:03.696 --> 00:37:08.291
When I'm virtual, I train myself to
look at the camera as I'm speaking.

00:37:08.291 --> 00:37:11.490
Because as I look at the camera,
it looks like I'm looking at you,

00:37:11.490 --> 00:37:14.918
rather than looking down at all of your
images that I see on the screen and

00:37:14.918 --> 00:37:17.110
thank you to those of
you showing your video.

00:37:18.380 --> 00:37:20.946
So, we want to make eye contact.

00:37:20.946 --> 00:37:23.575
And then finally,
when it comes to nonverbal presence,

00:37:23.575 --> 00:37:26.100
we have to think about our voice.

00:37:26.100 --> 00:37:29.496
Your voice is a wind instrument just
like a trumpet or clarinet, a saxophone,

00:37:29.496 --> 00:37:32.870
the more air you put through it,
the more you can do with it.

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:34.480
We need to vary our voice.

00:37:35.760 --> 00:37:37.990
Our brains are wired to habituate.

00:37:39.070 --> 00:37:40.540
Anything that stays the same.

00:37:40.540 --> 00:37:42.900
We stopped paying attention to it.

00:37:42.900 --> 00:37:47.390
So you need to vary your voice to
keep people involved and engaged.

00:37:47.390 --> 00:37:49.360
Now for some of us, that's hard.

00:37:49.360 --> 00:37:55.092
We speak in a more monotone, mano rate
way, a great tool to help you have

00:37:55.092 --> 00:38:02.155
more variation in your voice is to insert
emotive words, adjectives and adverbs.

00:38:02.155 --> 00:38:06.165
If you need some help come back to
campus we have this lovely adverb wall.

00:38:06.165 --> 00:38:07.964
Some of you have seen it on the campus,

00:38:07.964 --> 00:38:11.405
insert some of those adverbs
into what you're saying.

00:38:11.405 --> 00:38:16.180
I would never come to you and say,
I'm really excited to be here.

00:38:16.180 --> 00:38:20.443
No I would say I'm really excited to
be here those emotive words invite and

00:38:20.443 --> 00:38:22.524
invoke that variation in my voice.

00:38:22.524 --> 00:38:27.240
So to answer the question what comes
after minimally viable communication and

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:32.270
you have your message crafted in and
honed and dialed in for maximal value.

00:38:32.270 --> 00:38:35.856
You then have to think about
the channel through which you will push

00:38:35.856 --> 00:38:36.892
that message out.

00:38:36.892 --> 00:38:41.512
And then how do you deliver it in terms of
your nonverbal presence, big balanced and

00:38:41.512 --> 00:38:46.720
still make eye contact if you're in North
American culture, in vary your voice.

00:38:46.720 --> 00:38:50.245
That was a long winded answer, but
hopefully helpful to thinking about what

00:38:50.245 --> 00:38:52.734
comes next after this process
we talked about today.

00:38:55.165 --> 00:38:59.205
&gt;&gt; Perfect, yes and actually, you answered
another question that had said asked for

00:38:59.205 --> 00:39:02.230
some tips when it came to
virtual communication.

00:39:02.230 --> 00:39:03.417
So I think that
&gt;&gt; And

00:39:03.417 --> 00:39:05.158
I add a couple more though
if somebody wants to listen.

00:39:05.158 --> 00:39:06.699
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:39:06.699 --> 00:39:10.150
&gt;&gt; Let me share a couple other tips about
nonverbal about virtual communication.

00:39:10.150 --> 00:39:12.930
This is something that we have
all had to learn a lot about.

00:39:12.930 --> 00:39:16.725
And interestingly, some new exciting
research is coming out to help us.

00:39:16.725 --> 00:39:22.300
A couple things, one, you want to make
sure that you fill half your screen.

00:39:22.300 --> 00:39:26.030
Many of us as I alluded to earlier,
stand really far away from our cameras.

00:39:26.030 --> 00:39:29.040
We are wired to pay
attention to people's faces.

00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:32.190
We read a lot of information
of people's faces.

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:35.600
And if we can't see somebody's face,
we feel uncomfortable.

00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:40.730
So fill your screen with half your body,
so half the screen should be you.

00:39:41.850 --> 00:39:44.936
Second make sure that you are well lit.

00:39:44.936 --> 00:39:50.020
Again, if we want to see people's faces,
we have to be able to see you.

00:39:50.020 --> 00:39:54.917
Many people, Look like they are in the
witness protection programme when they do

00:39:54.917 --> 00:39:56.000
virtual presenting.

00:39:56.000 --> 00:39:59.178
They're in the dark, they're in
the shadows, light things up and

00:39:59.178 --> 00:40:01.030
it doesn't have to cost a lot of money.

00:40:01.030 --> 00:40:04.587
I mean, you can tell, if you talk to
any teenager, ask them about TikTok and

00:40:04.587 --> 00:40:06.367
ring lights, they'll wax poetic and

00:40:06.367 --> 00:40:09.606
they'll tell you how you can get
these different lights to light up.

00:40:09.606 --> 00:40:13.741
But you can actually just run down to a
hardware store, get some mechanics lights,

00:40:13.741 --> 00:40:17.639
the kind of lights a mechanic would clip
to your hood, to look at your engine and

00:40:17.639 --> 00:40:20.050
that will work just as well.

00:40:20.050 --> 00:40:24.140
Another thing is raise your camera up so
it's eye level with you.

00:40:24.140 --> 00:40:28.200
Many of you when we do virtual
communication we use laptops and

00:40:28.200 --> 00:40:33.950
the laptop typically sits lower than we
are so the angle is up under our chin.

00:40:33.950 --> 00:40:37.153
Nobody looks good from this angle,
in fact when I was a kid,

00:40:37.153 --> 00:40:41.387
if we wanted to scare people at night we
would shine a flashlight underneath our

00:40:41.387 --> 00:40:44.163
chin to look scary,
that's how some people look.

00:40:44.163 --> 00:40:48.354
Get a book, a box, a stand,
raise your laptop or your camera up so

00:40:48.354 --> 00:40:53.461
it's eye level with you, and then the last
bit of advice is many of these tools,

00:40:53.461 --> 00:40:56.630
Zoom, Meet, WebEx, et cetera, Teams.

00:40:56.630 --> 00:40:59.000
They have recording capabilities.

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:01.600
Record yourself practicing and watch it.

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:05.060
It is incredibly painful
to watch ourselves.

00:41:05.060 --> 00:41:08.977
We make all of our MBA students do it
in our strategic communication classes,

00:41:08.977 --> 00:41:11.567
they have to record and
watch themselves, it is yet

00:41:11.567 --> 00:41:14.940
the single best teacher to see
how you are communicating.

00:41:14.940 --> 00:41:18.936
So, make sure you can be seen, raise
your camera, get some good lighting and

00:41:18.936 --> 00:41:22.950
record yourself, that's going to help
you be a better virtual communicator.

00:41:25.300 --> 00:41:29.220
&gt;&gt; So next question is,
how has social media, Facebook, Twitter,

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:31.220
TikTok changed your concepts?

00:41:31.220 --> 00:41:34.420
Do you expect more changes
in the near future?

00:41:34.420 --> 00:41:37.279
&gt;&gt; Wow,
there's a lot that could be said here, so

00:41:37.279 --> 00:41:41.571
different modalities of communication
have clearly affected how we do

00:41:41.571 --> 00:41:45.900
strategic communication within
organizations for sure.

00:41:45.900 --> 00:41:49.270
And the one how ubiquitous they are.

00:41:49.270 --> 00:41:53.942
But also how quickly you can put messages
out there really necessitates this

00:41:53.942 --> 00:41:56.724
minimally viable communication approach.

00:41:56.724 --> 00:42:00.768
There will be times where you just
have to get messages out quickly and

00:42:00.768 --> 00:42:04.951
you can use these social media tools
as a way of testing your messages and

00:42:04.951 --> 00:42:08.150
as the endpoint of the process as well.

00:42:08.150 --> 00:42:11.948
We spend a lot of time in our strategic
communication classes talking about what's

00:42:11.948 --> 00:42:15.216
the appropriate channel for
the message you're trying to get across.

00:42:15.216 --> 00:42:20.046
And often, these different types of
social media and the other communication

00:42:20.046 --> 00:42:24.160
tools slack et cetera,
need to be considered as viable channels.

00:42:24.160 --> 00:42:26.221
So we have to think about,

00:42:26.221 --> 00:42:31.850
what does the channel bring to
the way in which we craft the message.

00:42:31.850 --> 00:42:34.750
If you're on Twitter,
you have constraints.

00:42:34.750 --> 00:42:37.390
How many words you can use,
you have to think about it.

00:42:37.390 --> 00:42:42.717
It does the message, make better sense to
have visual if you're using Instagram,

00:42:42.717 --> 00:42:48.710
for example, so it requires you to bring
in other questions in the design process.

00:42:48.710 --> 00:42:53.470
What I foresee coming forward more and
more, types of social channels for

00:42:53.470 --> 00:42:58.458
us to communicate in, I don't know how
many of you are aware of something like

00:42:58.458 --> 00:43:03.479
a clubhouse or other things where it's
an audio only social media platform.

00:43:03.479 --> 00:43:07.752
We're going to see more and more
opportunities for us to communicate and

00:43:07.752 --> 00:43:10.973
other tools and
we have to think about our messaging and

00:43:10.973 --> 00:43:15.828
make sure that our messaging is specific
and tailored to the different channels.

00:43:15.828 --> 00:43:20.386
Again, the context piece in that process
I went through for minimally viable

00:43:20.386 --> 00:43:24.890
communication is really about considering
those elements of the channel.

00:43:24.890 --> 00:43:26.480
So I'm excited by it.

00:43:26.480 --> 00:43:27.610
I think it's fascinating.

00:43:27.610 --> 00:43:29.910
I love teaching folks who
are younger than I am.

00:43:29.910 --> 00:43:32.990
I learn a lot about different
ways of communicating.

00:43:32.990 --> 00:43:35.810
And we all have to embrace
these different channels.

00:43:35.810 --> 00:43:40.338
Clearly, there are liabilities involved,
we have to think about it, there ethics

00:43:40.338 --> 00:43:44.609
involved, you need only look at the news
to see issues around social media, but

00:43:44.609 --> 00:43:48.435
they are channels for getting our
messages out and we have to explore and

00:43:48.435 --> 00:43:50.000
in some cases exploit them.

00:43:52.060 --> 00:43:55.386
&gt;&gt; You may have already answered
this by mentioning practice,

00:43:55.386 --> 00:43:59.465
iteration, watching your recordings
as embarrassing as that might be.

00:43:59.465 --> 00:44:00.184
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:44:00.184 --> 00:44:03.645
how do you train students to
speak with more confidence?

00:44:03.645 --> 00:44:05.760
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, yeah.

00:44:05.760 --> 00:44:09.190
So confidence is something I've
spent a lot of my career focused on.

00:44:09.190 --> 00:44:13.250
It's research I did all the way
back in undergrad and grad school.

00:44:13.250 --> 00:44:17.080
It is critical to focus on confidence.

00:44:17.080 --> 00:44:21.430
Confidence is the biggest barrier to
people sharing their opinions and

00:44:21.430 --> 00:44:25.997
voices and I firmly believe we need to
hear from as many people as possible,

00:44:25.997 --> 00:44:30.080
in our dialogue to understand and
be better at what we do.

00:44:30.080 --> 00:44:34.680
So to my mind, confidence is really
about focusing on two issues.

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:39.874
One is the symptoms that happen to us
physiologically and mentally when we

00:44:39.874 --> 00:44:46.430
get nervous in our communication, and then
looking at some of the sources of anxiety.

00:44:46.430 --> 00:44:49.145
And I invite you to check out
not only that book I wrote or

00:44:49.145 --> 00:44:52.217
the website no freaking speaking
to see lots of my thoughts and

00:44:52.217 --> 00:44:55.930
thoughts of others on this, but
let me give you just two quick examples.

00:44:55.930 --> 00:44:58.000
One in each camp.

00:44:58.000 --> 00:45:00.550
The symptoms, and
the sources to help build confidence.

00:45:01.950 --> 00:45:05.396
When it comes to symptoms,
our body is invoking the fight, or

00:45:05.396 --> 00:45:08.345
flight response to our communication.

00:45:08.345 --> 00:45:12.849
I believe and others who study this that
there's an evolutionary component to why

00:45:12.849 --> 00:45:17.090
we get nervous, our bodies experience
speaking in front of others as a threat

00:45:17.090 --> 00:45:20.171
and respond in kind and
I'm happy if we have time later.

00:45:20.171 --> 00:45:24.755
In this session or in other ways to go
more into that perspective why we see

00:45:24.755 --> 00:45:28.661
it as a threat, but as a result
of that threat we invoke fight or

00:45:28.661 --> 00:45:33.117
flight, our heart rate elevates,
we begin breathing more shallow.

00:45:33.117 --> 00:45:37.746
A lot of adrenaline is pumped which causes
us to shake and perspire et cetera.

00:45:37.746 --> 00:45:41.646
The single best thing you can do to
short circuit the fight or flight

00:45:41.646 --> 00:45:46.470
response invoked through your anxiety
around communication is deep breathing.

00:45:46.470 --> 00:45:51.670
Deep belly breaths can calm down and
slow down your autonomic nervous system.

00:45:51.670 --> 00:45:53.636
Now, I recently learned and this is,

00:45:53.636 --> 00:45:57.950
I love doing the thing fast talk smart
podcast because I learned so much.

00:45:57.950 --> 00:46:01.410
I had a cognitive neuroscientist
here at Stanford.

00:46:01.410 --> 00:46:05.058
His name is Andrew Huberman, super bright
guy if you ever get a chance to check out

00:46:05.058 --> 00:46:08.384
his work, he was a guest on the podcast
and we were talking about stress and

00:46:08.384 --> 00:46:09.990
how to reduce stress.

00:46:09.990 --> 00:46:12.410
And I said, well,
everybody says deep breathing helps.

00:46:12.410 --> 00:46:14.051
Is that true?
He goes, absolutely, but

00:46:14.051 --> 00:46:15.050
it's not the inhale.

00:46:16.540 --> 00:46:18.700
And that rocked my world for
years, decades.

00:46:18.700 --> 00:46:21.790
I've been telling people take a deep,
inhale a deep breath.

00:46:21.790 --> 00:46:25.566
It's not the inhale that helps you relax,
it's actually the exhale.

00:46:25.566 --> 00:46:29.475
So, the rule of thumb, or
shall I say the rule of lung is this,

00:46:29.475 --> 00:46:32.120
to use deep breath to calm yourself down.

00:46:32.120 --> 00:46:35.460
You want your exhale to be
twice as long as your inhale.

00:46:35.460 --> 00:46:37.602
And again,
if we have extra time now or later, or

00:46:37.602 --> 00:46:40.770
you can just listen to the podcast,
you'll learn why this is.

00:46:40.770 --> 00:46:43.857
But if you take a slow three
count in to calm yourself down,

00:46:43.857 --> 00:46:47.448
make sure that your exhale is six count
and you only have to do it two or

00:46:47.448 --> 00:46:50.290
three times to actually
take the benefits so.

00:46:50.290 --> 00:46:54.582
To manage symptoms, probably the best
thing you can do, deep breath, focus on

00:46:54.582 --> 00:46:58.809
the exhalation, from a symptoms point
of view, from a sources point of view,

00:46:58.809 --> 00:47:03.060
the things that actually make us nervous,
there's a lot that we can focus on.

00:47:04.070 --> 00:47:08.562
Some of you might have heard me deliver a
talk that I did at a reunion several years

00:47:08.562 --> 00:47:10.720
ago now called Think Fast Talk Smart.

00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:13.293
The name of the podcast
came from that talk,

00:47:13.293 --> 00:47:17.884
where I talk about one source of anxiety
which has to do with the fact that many of

00:47:17.884 --> 00:47:22.130
us are made nervous by the goals
we're trying to achieve.

00:47:22.130 --> 00:47:23.786
Whenever you communicate, you have a goal.

00:47:23.786 --> 00:47:26.240
In fact, I encourage you to create a goal.

00:47:26.240 --> 00:47:30.570
And many of us can be made nervous by
the thought of not achieving that goal.

00:47:31.750 --> 00:47:35.670
So we begin to get nervous about
a potential negative future outcome.

00:47:35.670 --> 00:47:36.976
I'm not going to get the good grade.

00:47:36.976 --> 00:47:37.970
I'm not going to get the funding.

00:47:37.970 --> 00:47:39.610
I'm not going to get the support.

00:47:39.610 --> 00:47:42.670
We're worried about not
achieving our goal.

00:47:42.670 --> 00:47:47.729
That's the source of the anxiety, a way to
mitigate that source, to reduce the impact

00:47:47.729 --> 00:47:52.790
of that negative potential future
outcome is to become present oriented.

00:47:52.790 --> 00:47:57.420
And in that video, I talk a lot about what
you can do to become present oriented.

00:47:57.420 --> 00:48:02.555
For example, do something physical, take a
walk, do some exercise, listen to a song,

00:48:02.555 --> 00:48:06.936
or a playlist, or the one that I like to
encourage people to do, is to start at

00:48:06.936 --> 00:48:11.740
the number 100 and count backwards by
a challenging number like 13s or 17s.

00:48:13.110 --> 00:48:16.648
All of those activities bring
you into the present moment,

00:48:16.648 --> 00:48:21.010
which distract you from worrying
about a future consequence.

00:48:21.010 --> 00:48:23.130
So, how do you get more confident?

00:48:23.130 --> 00:48:25.460
You tackle both your symptoms and
your sources.

00:48:25.460 --> 00:48:27.110
And there's a lot more
I could say about that.

00:48:27.110 --> 00:48:31.080
But those are examples, I give you one
example of how to manage a symptom by deep

00:48:31.080 --> 00:48:34.768
breathing and how to manage a source
trying to become present oriented.

00:48:34.768 --> 00:48:37.220
Long-winded answer, but
a really important issue.

00:48:38.250 --> 00:48:42.716
&gt;&gt; If you're doing a presentation for
which there is a somewhat standardized set

00:48:42.716 --> 00:48:46.370
of expectations for the format or
the content, so for example,

00:48:46.370 --> 00:48:51.260
a start-up team is pitching a potential
investor adopt your approach.

00:48:51.260 --> 00:48:55.426
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so there are often situations
where there are expectations for

00:48:55.426 --> 00:48:59.027
the structure of the communication
like an investor pitch or

00:48:59.027 --> 00:49:02.203
you're presenting your research or
your findings,

00:49:02.203 --> 00:49:06.246
there are often expectations around
the structure of the message.

00:49:06.246 --> 00:49:10.171
Yet the content that goes into
that message I think would benefit

00:49:10.171 --> 00:49:13.877
very directly from what we talked
about in terms of minimally

00:49:13.877 --> 00:49:16.020
viable communication design.

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:17.879
So the structure might be set.

00:49:17.879 --> 00:49:19.309
So for example in a pitch deck,

00:49:19.309 --> 00:49:22.775
there's an expectation that you'll
start by talking about the problem,

00:49:22.775 --> 00:49:27.650
you'll introduce your team, you'll talk
about the total addressable market, etc.

00:49:27.650 --> 00:49:31.513
But how you approach that,
what you say about that market, or

00:49:31.513 --> 00:49:36.417
how you define and frame the problem,
all of that can be rapidly prototyped and

00:49:36.417 --> 00:49:40.450
tested to figure out the best way for
this particular audience.

00:49:40.450 --> 00:49:44.116
So I think that those structures
can actually be helpful going into

00:49:44.116 --> 00:49:48.435
a pitch where the person says, in your
pitch, you must do these seven things,

00:49:48.435 --> 00:49:50.221
that's wonderfully helpful.

00:49:50.221 --> 00:49:54.862
That's very insightful information about,
here, the person is telling me how they

00:49:54.862 --> 00:49:58.416
want to hear my message,
they are telling me how to structure it.

00:49:58.416 --> 00:50:02.040
Great, now I'm going to iterate
as best I can to figure out for

00:50:02.040 --> 00:50:06.434
each one of those identified steps,
slides or parts of the presentation,

00:50:06.434 --> 00:50:10.220
how can I maximize the value of
what I say for each of those steps.

00:50:10.220 --> 00:50:15.875
So I actually think the structure given
can be very helpful to the process

00:50:15.875 --> 00:50:21.060
we walk through
&gt;&gt; This may be related to that but

00:50:21.060 --> 00:50:26.144
is the call to action component required
and the example given here is that

00:50:26.144 --> 00:50:31.146
our alum as a volunteer in the National
Park likes to import knowledge and

00:50:31.146 --> 00:50:36.066
perhaps some emotion but he feels it
might be a little too preachy when he

00:50:36.066 --> 00:50:39.674
gives us action, so any thought for-
&gt;&gt; First, thank you for

00:50:39.674 --> 00:50:41.160
the work you do in the National Parks.

00:50:41.160 --> 00:50:44.629
I and my family have benefited from,
I mean, enjoyed going in and

00:50:44.629 --> 00:50:49.050
learning from the people who are there,
so thank you for that work.

00:50:49.050 --> 00:50:51.723
You don't always have to
have a call to action.

00:50:51.723 --> 00:50:55.950
I would like for you though to consider,
should I have a call to action?

00:50:55.950 --> 00:50:58.920
What are the advantages and
disadvantages of doing that?

00:50:58.920 --> 00:51:00.888
And then make a judgment call.

00:51:00.888 --> 00:51:02.745
So I share in the process,

00:51:02.745 --> 00:51:07.925
I introduced the idea of where
you would put one, that now what.

00:51:07.925 --> 00:51:09.635
But you don't always have to invoke it.

00:51:09.635 --> 00:51:14.156
Again, you know your audience, you know
who you are and whether it works best for

00:51:14.156 --> 00:51:18.367
you, but we'd like you to consider what
would a call to action sound like.

00:51:18.367 --> 00:51:22.763
And there are calls to action that
are less directive and less preachy.

00:51:22.763 --> 00:51:26.655
It could simply be thank you for your time
and for showing your passion for nature

00:51:26.655 --> 00:51:30.374
and the environment, and I encourage
you to keep focus on the environment,

00:51:30.374 --> 00:51:34.400
it's very important, that's a very
soft way of giving a call to action.

00:51:34.400 --> 00:51:39.152
So again, it's not always required, I do
think it's important to think should I and

00:51:39.152 --> 00:51:43.178
if I were to do that what would it sound
like and what are the advantages and

00:51:43.178 --> 00:51:44.770
disadvantages of doing so.

00:51:44.770 --> 00:51:45.880
&gt;&gt; Perfect.

00:51:45.880 --> 00:51:48.060
&gt;&gt; A couple of quick set of questions.

00:51:48.060 --> 00:51:50.949
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; Facts, standing versus sitting,

00:51:50.949 --> 00:51:52.750
what do you choose?

00:51:52.750 --> 00:51:55.650
&gt;&gt; I choose standing, I'm standing now.

00:51:55.650 --> 00:51:57.540
I stand for two reasons.

00:51:57.540 --> 00:52:02.260
One, it helps me focus,
when I sit I get distracted more easily.

00:52:02.260 --> 00:52:07.758
And two, standing allows me to use my full
body more, it allows me to be taller so

00:52:07.758 --> 00:52:11.858
I can get more air into my lungs and
I find it helps me with what

00:52:11.858 --> 00:52:17.340
I call vocal stamina, the ability
to speak for long periods of time.

00:52:17.340 --> 00:52:21.180
If you stand though,
I recommend a couple things, one,

00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:24.910
you want to make sure that sway and
rock and lean.

00:52:24.910 --> 00:52:29.199
So it comes down to your foot position,
you want your feet to be parallel,

00:52:29.199 --> 00:52:30.937
underneath your shoulders.

00:52:30.937 --> 00:52:33.369
If your feet are splayed
out like a penguin or

00:52:33.369 --> 00:52:36.653
a duck which is how many of us stand,
it opens up your hips and

00:52:36.653 --> 00:52:39.575
it invites this swaying
which is very distracting.

00:52:39.575 --> 00:52:44.342
If you know you do sway and
rock a bit when you present standing and

00:52:44.342 --> 00:52:50.623
you're doing it virtually, get a stool, or
a box, or a book, put one foot up on it.

00:52:50.623 --> 00:52:54.184
There is no way you will sway or
rock if you have one foot up on a stool,

00:52:54.184 --> 00:52:56.990
you'll literally fall over if you try.

00:52:56.990 --> 00:52:58.090
So I like to stand.

00:52:58.090 --> 00:52:59.550
And what else, if you sit, that's fine.

00:52:59.550 --> 00:53:04.069
The big thing about sitting is you want to
make sure you are in a chair that is

00:53:04.069 --> 00:53:09.126
either locked down or cannot swivel a rock
because many of us will sway or swivel.

00:53:09.126 --> 00:53:12.812
And many chairs have armrests and
when you're sitting,

00:53:12.812 --> 00:53:17.725
you look like you're a robot because your
hands are just naturally placed here so

00:53:17.725 --> 00:53:22.360
remove or lower those armrests so
you can use your arms more naturally.

00:53:22.360 --> 00:53:26.205
&gt;&gt; Great, and what are your thoughts
on one camera, multiple cameras?

00:53:26.205 --> 00:53:30.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So I am a simpleton.

00:53:30.290 --> 00:53:33.520
It is very hard for
me to deal with just one camera.

00:53:33.520 --> 00:53:37.327
If you are in a situation where
you have assistance in help,

00:53:37.327 --> 00:53:40.310
then multiple cameras
might be interesting.

00:53:40.310 --> 00:53:44.246
Again, it's novel, it can change
things up and change perspectives,

00:53:44.246 --> 00:53:45.840
it's hard to manage.

00:53:45.840 --> 00:53:48.109
I thought what you were going to ask,
not one or two cameras,

00:53:48.109 --> 00:53:50.645
I thought you were going to say one or
two screens.

00:53:50.645 --> 00:53:52.988
And I have a different opinion on screens,

00:53:52.988 --> 00:53:55.795
I do think having multiple
screens can be helpful.

00:53:55.795 --> 00:53:59.028
Today, I have two screens that
I'm able to manage and watch,

00:53:59.028 --> 00:54:02.012
that allow me to keep track of
where my presentation is and

00:54:02.012 --> 00:54:04.955
where you are in terms of how
you're moving through it.

00:54:04.955 --> 00:54:08.830
So screens are different,
you do need to practice, and

00:54:08.830 --> 00:54:14.530
get comfortable with them, but I myself
use multiple screens in only one camera.

00:54:16.270 --> 00:54:17.220
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much, Matt.

00:54:17.220 --> 00:54:18.891
In the last couple of minutes,

00:54:18.891 --> 00:54:23.329
I would like to ask you if you have any
good recommendations, books or resources,

00:54:23.329 --> 00:54:25.925
around increasing
confidence when speaking?

00:54:25.925 --> 00:54:30.746
And also, is there a way for us or
companies to engage you as a coach or

00:54:30.746 --> 00:54:31.769
consultant?

00:54:31.769 --> 00:54:33.259
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you for
all those questions.

00:54:33.259 --> 00:54:37.743
So I do believe the speaking up without
picking out book is a book that can

00:54:37.743 --> 00:54:42.606
be very helpful to people who want to
become better communicators especially

00:54:42.606 --> 00:54:44.131
building confidence.

00:54:44.131 --> 00:54:48.238
The book was written for that, the book
was written for Stanford MBA students in

00:54:48.238 --> 00:54:52.483
mind so those of you who have been here or
are here hopefully can benefit from that.

00:54:52.483 --> 00:54:57.251
The book I mentioned earlier when I
talked about the Don't Mess With Texas ad

00:54:57.251 --> 00:55:01.946
Made to Stick, I think is a wonderful
book around communication skills and

00:55:01.946 --> 00:55:03.309
can be very helpful.

00:55:03.309 --> 00:55:07.794
There are lots of videos and
resources out there to help,

00:55:07.794 --> 00:55:13.224
I've tried to curate a lot of those
on the no freaking speaking site.

00:55:13.224 --> 00:55:18.513
In terms of reaching out to me,
I am very happy to help alum and students

00:55:18.513 --> 00:55:24.360
in any way I can, my email address can
be found on the the GSB faculty website.

00:55:24.360 --> 00:55:30.060
It's also here, I'll put up my my screen
again so you can see my email address.

00:55:30.060 --> 00:55:32.092
But I'm very happy to reach out,

00:55:32.092 --> 00:55:35.510
I co-founded a consulting
practice called Bold Echo.

00:55:35.510 --> 00:55:39.380
We do this work out in the corporate
world if that can be of help.

00:55:39.380 --> 00:55:42.520
I really just encourage all of you
to focus on your communication.

00:55:42.520 --> 00:55:44.130
Communication is critical.

00:55:44.130 --> 00:55:47.239
We saw through the pandemic
how communication can be

00:55:47.239 --> 00:55:51.903
incredibly important to keeping our
businesses connected and thriving, and

00:55:51.903 --> 00:55:56.070
our personal lives connected and
thriving and I hope minimally viable

00:55:56.070 --> 00:56:00.190
communication that approach can
help you hone your communication.

00:56:00.190 --> 00:56:04.180
So thank you for listening, and if I
can be of service, please let me know.

